# What each profile's own SUnit suites score, as of the last time somebody
# looked.  Read by tests/run_profiles.sh, which `make test` runs.
#
# This is a RATCHET, not a target.  The rule is that neither number may fall:
# a suite that ran 633 tests must still run 633, and a suite that passed 633
# must still pass 633.  Both directions matter, and the first one matters
# more than it looks -- a package whose tests stop being FOUND reports a
# perfect score, and that is exactly the shape of the failure this file
# exists to catch.  The Chronology suites spent a while reporting nothing at
# all because four classes deadlocked the runner, and nothing noticed.
#
# Improving a number fails too, with a message saying so.  That is
# deliberate: an unrecorded improvement is an improvement nothing is
# protecting, and the next regression silently spends it.  Edit the line in
# the same commit that earns it.
#
# Every profile is at 100%.  Two of them get there with something excluded and
# each says so in its own text: pharo-announcements excludes WeakAnnouncerTest,
# and pharo-weak excludes the four Dictionary tests whose superclass lives in a
# package it does not load.
#
# pharo-weak was 12/32 until the profile was given the classes its subjects are
# written against -- Pharo's WeakSet is a Pharo Set subclass and this profile
# was running it on 1983's -- and until the collector stopped marking a
# context's stack above its stack pointer.
#
# pharo-collections runs Pharo's Set, Dictionary, IdentityDictionary, Bag and
# MethodDictionary against the package's OWN tests, 469 of them, and all 469
# pass.  It was 115 when they were first wired in, and the grind from there
# found faults in this system rather than in the tests: a block with a
# trailing period answered nil, Object>>copy did not send postCopy so every
# copy of a Pharo collection shared its storage, becomeForward: did not
# exist, and the collector refused ephemerons.  Two of those four were
# compiler and object-memory bugs that had nothing to do with collections.
#
# A profile with no SUnit at all (bluebook) is not listed: it has no
# TestCase, so there is nothing to ask it.
#
# lib/Library-Tests adds 45 tests of the 1983 library itself -- the numeric
# tower, the collections, strings and streams -- so every profile that
# requires st2026 gains them too.  They found three bugs on their first run:
# a negative large-integer LITERAL lost its sign, a literal wider than 64
# bits wrapped, and the parser dropped whatever followed a complete method
# body instead of refusing it.
#
# lib/Database-Tests adds 43 more, and every number below moved by exactly 43
# for the same reason: they all require st2026.  A row that moved by anything
# else would be the interesting case, and there isn't one.
#
# Those 43 are the half of the database work that needs no database -- the
# join-path search, the SQL the query builder generates, and the exact
# decimals.  They found two real faults on their first run.  The scanner that
# reads table names out of an expression skipped every parenthesised group as
# a subquery, so COUNT(employee.employee_id) hid the table being counted, and
# the query joined nothing and answered a count over a cross product -- SQL
# that runs, returns numbers, and is wrong.  And a join to an ALIASED table
# wrote the real table name in its ON clause, referring to a table the FROM
# clause never introduced.
#
# The half that DOES need a database is not counted here and must not be:
# lib/Database-Live-Tests needs an ODBC driver manager and a driver, which a
# machine building this system cannot be assumed to have.  A suite that
# cannot run where it is checked would report a perfect score every time,
# which is the exact shape of failure this file exists to catch.  It has a
# profile of its own -- profiles/database-live.profile, 129 tests through
# SQLite -- and is run deliberately.
#
# profile               run  passed
st2026                  104  104
pharo-announcements     135  135
pharo-time              725  725
pharo-weak              124  124
pharo-collections       561  561
